Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Scarab Hatchery**, a squat, wide Duneforged hive-mound
whose central iris hatch turns and whose side vent bobs, as a 60x40x60 opaque-voxel
model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting discrete opaque
cells** one recorded operation at a time, and you **author its two decorative
animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the hatchery is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break
the building into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every form
are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a living hive-mound needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call **records only** — it
appends the operation to that part's log and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render`
when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews (`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled
scene.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a
parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a
**part** with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is
sculpted separately in the shared volume's coordinates.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name base
voxel-anim fill-box --part base --x0 6 --y0 0 --z0 6 --x1 49 --y1 22 --z1 49 --color "#c69a4b"
voxel-anim add-sphere --part hatch --cx 28 --cy 22 --cz 40 --r 8 --color "#565c64"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining parts,
setting pivots, defining joints, and defining animations and adding keyframes), and
`voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the
authoritative contract. **Run `voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see
that part's progress **and the assembled-scene previews under `scene/`
(`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole
hatchery composed from all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene
views are how you confirm the parts actually fit together: that the hatch sits centered on the
crown of the mound and the vent seats into its side. Because each part is sculpted in the
shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished building;
the scene simply composes them there.

